REGISTRY.satellite -- every code satellite uses, and what it means. 16 bits.

CHARACTERS AND TOKENS ARE TWO ALPHABETS (the author, 2026-09-16).

THE CODES ARE 16 BITS, AND THE HIGH BYTE SAYS WHICH (the author, 2026-09-16):

    0000000000000000 - 0000000001111111       0-127    the characters
    0000000010000000 - 0000000011111111     128-255    kept back, never issued
    0000000100000000 - 0000101111111111    256-3071    the tokens, 256 to a family
    0000110000000000 - 0000111111111111   3072-4095    free, and a token may grow into it
    0001000000000000 - 0001111111111111   4096-8191    THE WORDS, and nothing else
    0010000000000000 and up                  8192 up    free

so a high byte of zero is a character and any other high byte is a token. One
test, everywhere, forever -- the 8-bit registry's rule, widened. Because 128-255
are kept back, an 8-bit ASCII string widens into this by zero-extending each
code, and nothing is renumbered.

THE CHARACTERS are codes 0-127, and a string is the same at every width: an
8-BIT STRING HOLDS ASCII ONLY (the author: "8-bit will be ascii only then"), and a
16-bit string holds everything -- each character above 127 its own Unicode number,
except a wide one: above U+FFFF, or U+9C40 itself, it is 40000 and then its number
in two codes (D3.1, the author 2026-09-17: "its the 16-bit value for wide").

THE TOKENS are the stored program's alphabet, never a character and never inside
a string's value. A string literal's characters travel in the program behind
string_token.

EVERY PAYLOAD TOKEN CARRIES A COUNT, and this is the rule the 8-bit registry did
not need. At 8 bits a run of characters ended when the high bit flipped. That
test is arithmetically FALSE at 16 bits: above 127 a character IS its Unicode
number (satellite_string.cpp:53), so a string holding one wide character carries
codes indistinguishable from token codes -- QUAD writes " (infinity)  " as one
literal and would end its own string early. So a count follows every one of
family 0000100100000000's tokens, and the counted codes are SKIPPED, never
classified. long_count_token continues a count that does not fit in 16 bits, so
no literal has a ceiling (DESIGN §1.2 forbids one).

THE HIGH BYTE ALSO NAMES THE FAMILY, 256 to a family. 0000001100000000 up is
always arithmetic, 0000100100000000 up is always a literal. One indirect jump
classifies a token, and a family grows into its own 255 spare codes without any
other code moving. The character order moved twice in two days (f90fb12,
1311aec) and cost three files each time; the tokens are laid out so that never
happens again.

EVERY satellite.something.something HAS ITS OWN CODE, 4096 to 8191 (the author,
2026-09-16). 4096 itself is reserved and no word has it; the words run from 4097.
**words/words.tsv is what assigns them** -- a word's code is 4097 plus its row
there, and satellite/bytecode/make_word_codes.py generates the header from it, so
this file does not carry 4096 rows it would only be able to get wrong. 004 has
384 words, which leaves 3711 codes for the ones a user defines. The words sit at
4096 and not lower because the eleven token families reach 3071, and a range that
starts under them would have moved seven of them.

AND THE RANGE IS A FAST PATH, NEVER A CEILING. A word with no code in it still
travels as word_number_token and its numbers, which has no limit at all -- so
"the language has no limits" survives a 4096th word. Whatever reads a program
must handle both spellings of the same word.

00000000 nothing is reserved, as the author chose, so a zeroed or half-written
buffer reads as nothing rather than as a row of a's. 1111111111111111 is kept
back: it says the next code carries the token.

The order of the characters is the author's (2026-09-16), and comparing codes
sorts text in that order: a before z before A before 0 before ! before space.

ROWS MARKED "QUESTION" ARE NOT SETTLED. The author named these tokens on
2026-09-16; 003's DESIGN.md refuses or leaves open nineteen of them, verified
file and line by a review the same day. They keep their codes until the author
rules, and the reason stands beside each one. Nothing may rely on a QUESTION row.

ONE FILE, ONE ANSWER. The lexer, the interpreter and the .satc writer all read
this registry, so none of them can drift about what a code means. Like 003's
words.def, it is exempt from the 300-line rule.

